            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Background: Runnable Doesn't Mean Profitable</h2>
<p>A common frustration in Meta advertising: an ad creative has a great CTR and low CPC, yet generates almost no orders. This is the classic "runnable but not sellable" trap—a creative that tricks the algorithm into giving it impressions but fails to convince users to buy.</p>
<p>Without distinguishing between these two concepts during reviews, two outcomes follow: either you scale up a creative based on misleading metrics, watching your ROAS collapse, or you prematurely kill a potentially profitable creative.</p>
<p>This SOP applies to bi-weekly or monthly creative review sessions. Follow the steps below.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Filter Metrics and Categorize Creatives</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Goal: Score each creative on both "runnability" and "sellability."</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Collect These 4 Core Metrics</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Formula</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>Clicks / Impressions</td>
<td>Measures "runnability": creative appeal to the algorithm</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPC</td>
<td>Spend / Clicks</td>
<td>Measures "runnability": efficiency of attraction</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR</td>
<td>Conversions / Clicks</td>
<td>Measures "sellability": actual purchase intent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>Spend / Conversions</td>
<td>Measures "sellability": overall conversion efficiency</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>分流 Creatives into a Four-Quadrant Matrix</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Top Performers</strong> (High CTR + High CVR): Runnable AND sellable → Scale budget</li>
<li><strong>Engagement Traps</strong> (High CTR + Low CVR): Clicks but no conversions → Optimize landing page</li>
<li><strong>Hidden Gems</strong> (Low CTR + High CVR): Few clicks but high intent → Test new thumbnails/first 3 seconds</li>
<li><strong>Kill List</strong> (Low CTR + Low CVR): Neither runs nor sells → Pause or redesign</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: Use industry benchmarks or your previous period's data as baseline. Above baseline = "High," below = "Low."</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Diagnose Root Causes for "Runnable but Not Sellable"</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Goal: Identify why a creative fools the algorithm but fails to convert users.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>3 Common Root Causes + Checklist</h3>
<p><strong>① Induced clicks without purchase intent</strong><br />
- Are headlines/thumbnails overly sensational ("Free!" "Limited time!") creating expectation gaps?<br />
- Does the headline focus on core product value rather than just hype?</p>
<p><strong>② Disconnected landing page experience</strong><br />
- Does the landing page match the ad's promise?<br />
- Is the purchase flow within 3 steps? Is the "Add to Cart" button prominent?</p>
<p><strong>③ Audience overlap or fatigue</strong><br />
- Are multiple creatives for the same product competing for the same users?<br />
- Has this creative been running for over 21 days, entering fatigue?</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: Mark each identified root cause with a "Root Cause ID" in your review sheet for tracking optimization results.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Assign Differentiated Actions</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Goal: Match each creative with the optimal next step—no blanket pausing or scaling.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Creative Status → Action Reference</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Status</th>
<th>Action</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>High CTR + High CVR</td>
<td>+30-50% budget</td>
<td>Verify scale ceiling before further increases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High CTR + Low CVR</td>
<td>A/B test landing page</td>
<td>Prioritize testing detail page headline, hero image, CTA copy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Low CTR + High CVR</td>
<td>Test new thumbnail/first 3s</td>
<td>Keep conversion path unchanged, improve entry creative only</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Low CTR + Low CVR</td>
<td>Pause and archive</td>
<td>Retain data for future reference—never delete</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Pre-Scale Risk Checks</h3>
<p>Before scaling a "runnable AND sellable" creative, complete these checks:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Is overall account ROAS within target range?</li>
<li>[ ] Does this creative's daily spend exceed 30% of account daily budget (avoid single-creative dominance)?</li>
<li>[ ] Has this creative accumulated at least 50 conversion events (ensure statistical confidence)?</li>
<li>[ ] Are new creatives for this product in testing (prepare for succession)?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Checklist: Mandatory for Every Review</h2>
<ol>
<li>Is four-quadrant data updated to the latest period?</li>
<li>Has each creative been marked with "runnability" and "sellability" ratings?</li>
<li>Have root causes been identified for all "runnable but not sellable" creatives?</li>
<li>Have action items been communicated to responsible designers or media buyers?</li>
<li>Have all budget-increased creatives passed risk checks?</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>Creative reviews aren't about finding "good creatives"—they're about finding "profitable creatives." Being runnable is the threshold; being sellable is the goal. Master this SOP and media buyers can complete a meaningful creative review in 30 minutes instead of staring at reports blankly.</p>
